    "done_when": "Read section 31 of crew-runtime-positive-controls first. In reckon/_backends.py, around protected_read_only_binds and the --ro-bind loop that uses it, a launch whose protected read-only bind source is absent at the moment the fence is built waits and retries a bounded number of times, with the count and the interval as named module constants, and launches only once every protected source exists. A source that stays absent past the bound refuses the launch, naming the path, and the launch never proceeds with that path unbound or writable. The source can vanish between the check and the sandbox start, because ~/.claude.json is rewritten by replacement. Handle that window too: either retry the sandbox start when it fails with the bind error naming a protected path, or show in the evidence why the window cannot occur. tests/test_protected_bind_waits_for_its_source.py uses temporary paths only and never the real home. A protected file that appears after a short delay leads to a successful launch, and a protected file that never appears leads to a refusal naming it. Existing tests that reach protected_read_only_binds keep passing; grep tests/ for it to build the gate. Record the outcome in docs/evidence/fragments/crew-runtime-positive-controls/protected-bind-waits-for-its-source.html. Run `date -u` first and treat that instant as your start; your deadline is 60 minutes later. Write your manifest before starting any long command. Run both gate arms with pytest-xdist (-n 8 --timeout=300), never serially. Run the base arm, from a `git archive` extraction of your base under /tmp in a directory named for this run, and then the head arm, each in the foreground: a background command dies when your turn ends. Keep the gate to the test files that reach your change, tens of files rather than hundreds. Each arm uses its own basetemp outside any repository and writes to its own new log path, which it never overwrites. Each arm is recorded as JSON carrying completed 